# Line endings.
#
# Two families of test read files in this repo as bytes and assert on `\n`,
# and a Windows checkout under the default `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites them
# to CRLF, so both fail for a reason that has nothing to do with what they
# test:
#
#   - The telemetry golden fixtures are compared byte for byte against a
#     string a generator builds with `\n` (`pipe::tests::golden`), and the
#     Node oracle in `crates/offline-protocol/tests/tools/` reads the same
#     files.
#   - The bridge source guards in `offline-protocol-uniffi` read the Swift,
#     Kotlin, TypeScript and UDL sources and match multi-line snippets in
#     them, which is how the hand-mirrored constants and the lifecycle
#     wiring are pinned across three languages (docs/bridges/README.md C5).
#     Dozens of tests do this, and the count grows with every pinned
#     invariant, so it is deliberately not stated here as a number that would
#     go stale unpinned. Several were only ever exercised on Unix runners,
#     because `cargo test` stops at the first failing binary and an earlier
#     crate always failed on Windows first.
#   - Generated Rust checked in beside its generator (the C binding's
#     dispatcher, `capi/generated.rs`) is compared byte for byte with what
#     the generator builds with `\n`, by `the_generated_c_dispatch_is_current`
#     and by `tools/capi-dispatch-gen --check`. Pinning `*.rs` rather than
#     that one file covers the next guard that reads Rust source too.
#
# Pinning these to LF makes the bytes on disk the bytes the tests expect on
# every platform. Nothing in the toolchain (cargo, gradle, node, Xcode) wants
# CRLF, so this costs nothing.
*.json text eol=lf
*.swift text eol=lf
*.kt text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.udl text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.podspec text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
*.m text eol=lf
